**Read:** Complete the activated-sludge portion of [Activated Sludge and Aerobic Biofilm Reactors — von Sperling](../sources/Activated-Sludge-and-Aerobic-Biofilm-Reactors.lingua#r_0WMGVEM60J63M01SJJDQ9357GE); work [Water and Wastewater Treatment Engineering — MIT 1.85](../sources/Water-and-Wastewater-Treatment-Engineering-MIT.lingua#r_396YEC55H29CR6J4ZBZX5JBY60), lectures 17–18 and associated problems. Continue with [Biological Wastewater Treatment — Chen and colleagues, second edition](../sources/Biological-Wastewater-Treatment-Chen-and-Colleagues.lingua#r_1AE9DK0YWQR5GDECZ9KK1JJP37) for more detailed reactor and settling models.

Follow influent, mixed liquor, clarified effluent, return activated sludge and waste activated sludge. Returning solids supports a biomass inventory; wasting and effluent loss determine its departure. The reactor and separator constrain one another. Increasing return flow is not a universal cure for poor settling or low biological activity.

For an original ideal separator with no effluent solids and no waste drawn from its underflow, let incoming fresh flow be Q, return flow RQ, mixed-liquor concentration X and return concentration Xr. Solids balance gives (1 + R)X = RXr, so R = X/(Xr − X). With X = 3 and Xr = 9 kg/m³, R = 0.5. Introducing wasting, effluent solids or inventory change requires the full balance.

**Work:** Draw the hydraulic and solids accounts for two wasting locations and identify which flow contains which concentration. Diagnose a synthetic case where ammonia removal deteriorates while effluent suspended solids also rise: list competing biological and separation explanations and evidence to distinguish them. Extend to transient inventory and sensor uncertainty before proposing control changes.

**Continue:** [Settling flux, blankets and the limits of a single index](Settling-flux-blankets-and-the-limits-of-a-single-index.lingua#r_1MAYFYXBANP0CPDB34EJPEGT05).
